#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151967 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7223f4c-b6aa-5e20-8f6b-1f333bac0da6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Velocity"}] :question/id #uuid "511feb42-d0dd-55b6-85c5-f0605fcf8da4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9454" :question/problem "A body of mass $3 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}).$\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) =t^2- e^{t-4}$ meters per second.\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=4$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-{e}^{t-4}) \\\\  & =2t-{e}^{t-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=4 \\, \\textrm{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =2(4)-{e}^{4-4} \\\\  & =8-1 \\\\  & =7\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot (7) \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F| = |21| = 21 \\, \\textrm{N}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151976 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e955e35e-c45f-53b9-9f95-332d3e9c648f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Displacement"}] :question/id #uuid "3ecb82f0-3877-5732-851d-adec73abb890" :question/math-academy-id "e-9859" :question/problem "A body of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t) = e^{3t-6} + t^2$ meters. Find the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t-6}+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{3t-6}+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{e}^{3t-6}+2t) \\\\  & =9{e}^{3t-6}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force acting on the body, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =2\\cdot (9{e}^{3t-6}+2) \\\\  & =18{e}^{3t-6}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151977 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/id #uuid "2666ce7a-356a-574a-88fd-97417afc8ada" :question/math-academy-id "e-9860" :question/problem "A body of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ moves in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\geq 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) =2t^3-12t^2-30t+5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time when the force acting on the body has a magnitude of zero." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}-30t+5) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-24t-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-24t-30 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-4t-5 & =0 \\\\ (t+1)(t-5) & =0 \\\\ t & =-1,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t \\geq 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude when $t=5 \\, \\textrm{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b58342de-c454-5afe-9ecb-ccafd98db48e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 5s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149059} {:answer/id #uuid "d43ff081-53ff-551e-9f2a-b0dc4ac8ae7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149060} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b2fc05-bb67-59ca-b877-9588be0e5e39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 3s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149061} {:answer/id #uuid "1eda97be-7632-50f1-89a9-15ea96362e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 1s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149062} {:answer/id #uuid "7484898e-4401-56e2-b0e3-591df12b1c53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 6s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7b2fc05-bb67-59ca-b877-9588be0e5e39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 3s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149061} :answer-field/id #uuid "977bf223-9796-5239-a7a2-1373f812b958" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "396d3531-2c0c-5e02-bb04-58dd2fc7f372" :question/math-academy-id "q-138769" :question/problem "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-36t+2$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-36t+2) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-6t-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-6t-36 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-t-6 & =0 \\\\ (t-3)(t+2) & =0 \\\\ t & =-2,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5048da6-f06e-54ef-99f3-02d35784d53e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 7s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149065} {:answer/id #uuid "f141134b-33f4-5535-a223-d62b5f9b2448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 3s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149066} {:answer/id #uuid "06ce788d-868d-5cf0-b75c-d08a7ee4d31f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 4s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149067} {:answer/id #uuid "55869669-22e0-5ae3-a4b8-017647f5cb30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149068} {:answer/id #uuid "60a06afb-6930-5bb6-9a10-096b29ce56c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 5s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55869669-22e0-5ae3-a4b8-017647f5cb30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149068} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca7066d7-ace6-51fb-8248-23eb546ef0b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8295ff34-59d6-595e-a3a0-97b1c540771c" :question/math-academy-id "q-138770" :question/problem "A body of mass $1.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=2{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}+t-5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}+t-5) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-24t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}-24t+1) \\\\  & =12t-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12t-24 & =0 \\\\ 12 & =24 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7223f4c-b6aa-5e20-8f6b-1f333bac0da6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9d5b3d4-64c0-5d57-817a-4225cbe120b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372652} {:answer/id #uuid "efa19a68-6b95-5060-b453-8623e1560f27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372653} {:answer/id #uuid "3c1fc86d-1ed1-5f78-aca6-a90bfedecf2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372654} {:answer/id #uuid "75e68ff6-a34e-58e9-9f7c-c4882bcd1618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372655} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb21bf7-89ea-5846-a793-743ffe5ebf58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75e68ff6-a34e-58e9-9f7c-c4882bcd1618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372655} :answer-field/id #uuid "0abc5fc0-d2da-5fa8-8173-bde814c17f00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c20835d-7bd8-5e9c-8ea5-8bdbf915a1be" :question/math-academy-id "q-138889" :question/problem "A body of mass $8\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=4\\sqrt{t}-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3)$ meters per second.\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=4$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\sqrt{t}-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}-\\frac{5}{t-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=4\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4}}-\\frac{5}{4-3} \\\\  & =1-5 \\\\  & =-4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F|=|-32|=32\\,\\text{N}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e955e35e-c45f-53b9-9f95-332d3e9c648f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40a6fefa-fb2e-550e-8275-b40a631dd214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24{t}^{2}-12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372659} {:answer/id #uuid "ed574d9a-666c-561a-88d5-73583042eff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{t}^{2}-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372660} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b1cd78-6a23-5b92-aa57-b6fe312dc7c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{t}^{2}-9t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372661} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5132e9-fe86-5b0e-8ba3-3e73526ccf9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{t}^{2}+12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372662} {:answer/id #uuid "4d02d2e4-8d88-5c7c-996a-11078f007106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{t}^{2}+6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3b1cd78-6a23-5b92-aa57-b6fe312dc7c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{t}^{2}-9t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372661} :answer-field/id #uuid "efaf7af2-7a96-562f-b508-266989de8149" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d0e326c-9947-5e66-959d-db32e939a7f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-138900" :question/problem "A body of mass $1.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={t}^{4}-{t}^{3}$ meters.\nFind the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =1.5\\cdot (12{t}^{2}-6t) \\\\  & =18{t}^{2}-9t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e955e35e-c45f-53b9-9f95-332d3e9c648f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "878beeac-d61b-5bf0-9a2b-6b41aff66d82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24{e}^{2t}-12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310664} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5325b3-7241-537f-a326-f60477e4a478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2t}-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310665} {:answer/id #uuid "184eaca3-31bb-5561-8cf6-c0f17fcc7727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{e}^{2t}-18t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310666} {:answer/id #uuid "ccbbd2ae-3131-53d6-a6ef-b221f80640ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{e}^{2t}-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310667} {:answer/id #uuid "78658f23-26dd-5b83-a1bc-29519a54624a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{e}^{2t}-12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "184eaca3-31bb-5561-8cf6-c0f17fcc7727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{e}^{2t}-18t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310666} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e3b8fc4-2210-5501-96fa-fe95a42ffe3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce8fb41e-d014-5085-a244-2a0981843cf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-138901" :question/problem "A body of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={e}^{2t}-{t}^{3}$ meters.\nFind the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{2t}-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{2t}-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot (4{e}^{2t}-6t) \\\\  & =12{e}^{2t}-18t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7223f4c-b6aa-5e20-8f6b-1f333bac0da6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e143678e-4dfa-51c9-bd27-7dbfe99013c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16t+12\\cos(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e143678e-4dfa-51c9-bd27-7dbfe99013c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16t+12\\cos(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372666} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e4b3527-f8b3-54b0-a13c-75d3e73ae462" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23bec17b-f4e5-5238-be97-5731032f5907" :question/math-academy-id "q-315648" :question/problem "A body of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=2{t}^{2}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ meters per second.  Find an expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =4t+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (4t+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =16t+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e955e35e-c45f-53b9-9f95-332d3e9c648f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1c128e2-f026-54cd-af03-9ccce3af6c0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1c128e2-f026-54cd-af03-9ccce3af6c0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372669} :answer-field/id #uuid "350ef28e-1c61-5ea8-aabf-a59f99fbf641" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "92d1e0da-6675-52b1-9f96-c4f5a35a7942" :question/math-academy-id "q-315735" :question/problem "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$  At time $t>0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={t}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ meters.\n\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=2$ seconds. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the force equals\n{{field-1}} $\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t+\\frac{4}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+\\frac{4}{t}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=2\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2) & =2-\\frac{4}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & =ma(2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F(2)|=|2|=2\\,\\text{N}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7223f4c-b6aa-5e20-8f6b-1f333bac0da6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92aff546-a8cf-506e-8cda-2773ca87f447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{t^2+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92aff546-a8cf-506e-8cda-2773ca87f447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{t^2+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372672} :answer-field/id #uuid "c72c1987-ec25-54f4-8b2c-27a961d701fa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45eb9b77-c6ce-5aa4-801c-a93678ce677f" :question/math-academy-id "q-345206" :question/problem "A body of mass $1\\,\\text{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+3)$ meters per second.  Find an expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7223f4c-b6aa-5e20-8f6b-1f333bac0da6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6be53d43-1d90-5c86-99f5-e9726f77f703" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372675} {:answer/id #uuid "8dfd070b-2a61-59f4-92b6-41c47413e794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8t}{\\sqrt{1-t^2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8dfd070b-2a61-59f4-92b6-41c47413e794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8t}{\\sqrt{1-t^2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372676} :answer-field/id #uuid "f36f7e29-def4-5e9b-b019-e4536d1d2707" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8a625fd-0a7f-5970-a294-68b09e11cf6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-345207" :question/problem "A body of mass $8\\,\\text{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=t\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)$ meters per second.  Find an expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=8\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+t\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+\\frac{8t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7223f4c-b6aa-5e20-8f6b-1f333bac0da6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c9172c6-2360-508f-8ace-832111819474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372679} {:answer/id #uuid "c09c9dd6-4afc-5e75-89df-a1fb2517f727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c09c9dd6-4afc-5e75-89df-a1fb2517f727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372680} :answer-field/id #uuid "761a5017-d38e-51fc-b9c8-f7283907b94a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f09fa8f-3853-500b-aee6-60e0f296f95d" :question/math-academy-id "q-345227" :question/problem "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=4{e}^{\\frac{t}{2}}$ meters per second. \n\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ seconds. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the force equals\n{{field-1}} $\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{e}^{\\frac{t}{2}}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\frac{t}{2}}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\frac{t}{2}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\frac{t}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =2{e}^{\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =2(3) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =ma(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)|=|1|=1\\,\\text{N}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e955e35e-c45f-53b9-9f95-332d3e9c648f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42e48301-3781-5b1f-ac80-f45f4f63437f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos(t)-t\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310716} {:answer/id #uuid "e678ad20-8680-52b6-9bc9-38874c470ecf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos(t)+t\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42e48301-3781-5b1f-ac80-f45f4f63437f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos(t)-t\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310716} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5b299de-f350-54a7-bf19-2252c4ffc75d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "485f113e-1072-57f0-bd8c-02699b28f536" :question/math-academy-id "q-345339" :question/problem "A body of mass $1\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e955e35e-c45f-53b9-9f95-332d3e9c648f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fefe3b53-f05d-512a-a0fe-9b2334b8e141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{(1+t^2)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359117} {:answer/id #uuid "1aeaa308-cd0e-5644-b253-9b3a050dda88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{t}{(1+t^2)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1aeaa308-cd0e-5644-b253-9b3a050dda88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{t}{(1+t^2)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359118} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5ae1eba-b1a1-511e-ba2d-0316cadded79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df1e6f0b-3cb8-5b84-956f-6e1aa296a8a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-345340" :question/problem "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-1}] \\\\  & =-{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14ca23df-bbe5-5690-ab97-8b20b52f69d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14ca23df-bbe5-5690-ab97-8b20b52f69d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372684} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa8f5773-6736-50db-875f-9e4bcfd65929" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "313dfc6b-b12e-5461-9869-6c76347df847" :question/math-academy-id "q-345382" :question/problem "A body of mass $1\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}-8t$ meters per second.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}}  $\\text{s}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}-8t) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ 2{t}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =4 \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=2\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7219c7c-0fa4-5cfa-b9cd-e9ff0de6076c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372687} {:answer/id #uuid "6243b63f-1ae0-59f4-969d-8ac6392776d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6243b63f-1ae0-59f4-969d-8ac6392776d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372688} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e6f0341-1d22-5355-a867-7e6d2804bf8b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "900029e6-9ba2-594b-a9ae-e4e625382d39" :question/math-academy-id "q-345388" :question/problem "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=(2t-9){e}^{-2t}$ meters per second.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}}  $\\text{s}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2t-9){e}^{-2t}] \\\\  & =(2t-9)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-2t})+{e}^{-2t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-9) \\\\  & =(2t-9)(-2{e}^{-2t})+2{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =(-4t+18){e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =(-4t+20){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4t+20){e}^{-2t} & =0 \\\\ -4t+20 & =0 \\\\ -4t & =-20 \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=5\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372689 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7129fe52-04fa-55b0-88be-0ec0c69995ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7129fe52-04fa-55b0-88be-0ec0c69995ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372691} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e63334d-e5db-5e4a-99c3-8002e96bb33c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae999be0-8621-57af-ac32-991271d83e5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-345391" :question/problem "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons. At time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+6t+12$ meters.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{s}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+6t+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-6t+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-6t+6) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+t-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+t-6 & =0 \\\\ (t-2)(t+3) & =0 \\\\ t & =-3,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=2\\,\\text{s}.$"}]}
